{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1578754422,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWU1NDg0Mzg5MzE=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/4011","origin":"github"},"title":"Establish and use severity- labels to prioritize issues/PRs","message":"Inspired by https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3808#issuecomment-573320479 .\nI think we might want to establish a set of tags to mark \"severity\" or importance (may be mimicing Debian but melding top 3 together so we get):\n\n- `severity-critical` - makes it unusable, causes data loss, introduces security vulnerability\n- `severity-important` - major effect on the usability of a package, without rendering it completely unusable to everyone\n- `severity-normal` - the default, majority of issues\n- `severity-minor` - \na problem which doesn't affect the package's usefulness, and is presumably trivial to fix\n- `severity-wishlist` - feature request, and also for any bugs that are very difficult to fix due to major design considerations `FOI:` etc\n- `severity-none` - for all the `FOI: ` and possibly some `Q: ` left unanswered\n\n`critical` and `important` would be release blockers, and all issues should be categorized accordingly.  Then you could easily filter to not miss any important one.","files":null}]}